### GENDER AND CHILD-BASED.  

# CHAPTER I

## GENERAL PROVISIONS

#### Article 1

In this Regional Regulation, what is meant by:

1. The Central Government hereinafter referred to as the Government is the President of the Republic of Indonesia who holds the governmental power of the State of the Republic of Indonesia as referred to in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia.  
2. Region is the Regency of Ogan Komering Ulu.  
3. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus as elements of regional government administration.  
4. Regent is the Regent of Ogan Komering Ulu.  
5. Child is a person who has not yet reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years, including a child still in the womb as referred to in Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection.  
6. Gender is a concept that refers to the roles and responsibilities of men and women that occur as a result of and can be changed by the social and cultural conditions of society.  
7. Protection is all efforts aimed at providing a sense of security and fulfilling the rights of victims carried out by family, integrated services, advocates, social institutions, police, prosecutors, courts, or other parties either temporarily or based on court determination.  
8. Child Protection is all activities to guarantee and protect children and their rights so that they can live, grow, develop, and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and worth, and receive protection from violence and discrimination.  
9. Protection implementation is a series of activities aimed at preventing the occurrence of violence, providing protection and recovery services and social reintegration, conducting coordination and cooperation, and increasing community participation carried out by the Commission for the Protection of Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children, and the Integrated Service Center.  
10. Gender-Based Violence is any form of restriction, exclusion, differentiation and all forms of treatment carried out on the basis of sex and aimed at reducing, eliminating the recognition, enjoyment or use of human rights, the consequences of which include but are not limited to physical, sexual, psychological and economic violence.  
11. Violence Against Children is any form of restriction, differentiation, exclusion and all forms of treatment carried out against children, the consequences of which include but are not limited to physical, sexual, psychological, and economic violence.  
12. Victim of gender-based violence is a person who because of their sex experiences physical, psychological, economic, social, sexual suffering, and other losses resulting from state policy, acts of violence and or threats of violence within the scope of the household and community.  
13. Child Victim of Violence is a child who receives harsh treatment both physically, psychologically, economically, socially, sexually, and other losses resulting from state policy, acts of violence and or threats of violence within the scope of the household and community.  
14. Victim Recovery is all efforts to strengthen victims of gender-based violence and violence against children to be more empowered, both physically, psychologically, socially, economically and sexually.  
15. Assistance is all actions in the form of counseling, psychological therapy, advocacy, and spiritual guidance, for the purpose of self-strengthening of victims of gender-based violence and violence against children to resolve the problems they face.  
16. Commission for the Protection of Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children hereinafter referred to as KPK2BGA is the Commission for the Protection of Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children of Ogan Komering Ulu Regency which is formed by the Regent and is a non-structural commission.  
17. Integrated Service is a series of activities to provide protection for victims of gender-based violence and children carried out jointly by relevant agencies or institutions as one unit of implementation, prevention efforts, health services, psychosocial rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration, and legal assistance for victims of gender-based violence and children.  
18. Social Reintegration is the process of preparing the community and victims that supports the reunification of victims into the family environment, family substitutes who can provide protection and fulfillment of the needs of victims.  
19. Safe House (shelter) is a temporary residence used to provide protection to victims in accordance with the determined operational standards.

# CHAPTER II

## PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

#### Article 2

Protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;  
b. best interests of the victim;  
c. justice and gender equality;  
d. victim protection;  
e. human survival;  
f. survival, growth, and development of children;  
g. respect for the opinion of children;  
h. transparency;  
i. integration;  
j. not blaming the victim;  
k. empowering;  
l. victim confidentiality; andm. decision-making in the hands of the victim.

#### Article 3

The objectives of protection of victims of gender-based violence and children are:
a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community; b. to provide protection;  
c. to provide legal assistance;  
d. to seek recovery and social reintegration; ande. to increase community participation.

# CHAPTER III

## RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

#### Article 4

1. Every victim of gender-based violence and child victim of violence has the right to:a. obtain protection for the security of themselves, their family, and their property, and be free from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;  
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;  
c. be free from ensnaring questions;  
d. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions;  
e. receive services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;  
f. recovery and social reintegration;  
g. obtain legal, psychological, spiritual, economic, social assistance and interpreters.  
2. The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER IV

## OBLIGATIONS OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

#### Article 5

1. The obligations of the Regional Government include:a. preventing the occurrence of violence;  
b. providing protection for victims of violence;  
c. providing recovery services and social reintegration;  
d. encouraging and increasing community participation;  
e. conducting cooperation with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence.  
2. To carry out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and children;  
b. establish integrated services and KPK2BGA;  
c. facilitate the implementation of integrated services and KPK2BGA activities;  
d. provide facilities and infrastructure;  
e. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;  
f. conduct coordination and cooperation in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and children; g. encourage community participation;  
h. conduct monitoring and evaluation.  
3. The regional government in carrying out obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

